id: pm-2evidence-dist
title: Compact retained runtime modules and stage packed SDK acceptance efficiently
description: "The combined literal-annotation, consistent-history and declared-recovery implementation produces a real npm pack receipt of 20,012,917 unpacked bytes, exceeding the unchanged 20,000,000-byte base limit. Existing whitespace compaction is already shipped; preserve all documentation, runtime paths, identifiers, public declarations and original source maps while eliminating redundant emitted syntax."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[3]: context-management,distribution,performance
created_at: "2026-10-04T04:41:29.139Z"
updated_at: "2026-10-04T08:12:38.128Z"
closed_at: "2026-10-04T08:10:29.940Z"
completed_at: "2026-10-04T08:10:29.940Z"
author: "harness:codex"
estimated_minutes: 60
acceptance_criteria: "Actual npm artifact stays below the unchanged 20 MB base budget;Real Node execution preserves exported names, successful values and exception semantics;Declarations and prebuilt bundles remain byte-identical;Original TypeScript source content remains in composed maps;Repeated finalization is stable;Installed npm npx and bunx acceptance remains green"
parent: pm-u9d0
risk: medium
confidence: high
resolution: "Retained runtime modules compact redundant syntax while preserving identifiers, behavior, declarations, bundles and source maps; packed SDK continuation stages actual publish selectors before real npm packing."
expected_result: Keep the actual npm artifact within the unchanged 20 MB ceiling and retain all public installed-consumer behavior without weakening packing or test time limits.
actual_result: "Runtime/negative-control and all eleven packed continuation scenarios passed; npm/npx/bunx smoke passed with nine extension packages and the artifact measured 19,893,321 bytes against 20,000,000. Shared linked execution passed four unique commands with three duplicate links explicitly skipped. Complete source suite: 9686 passed, 2 Windows-only Linux skips and exact 100/100/100/100 coverage."
